buffer_cache: a refused buffer no longer ends the session

Runs 51, 52 and 53 ended in BufferCache::CreateBuffer with an uncaught vk::Exception: 21, 17 and
10 MiB refused, the pool's largest block 1 to 6 MiB, and in run 53 the largest hole in the
allocator's blocks 6 MiB of about 200 MiB of holes. 0089, 0095 and 0096 took the staging buffers out
of this; nothing
catches a cached buffer. A join of overlapping buffers is one VkBuffer, so waiting does not make
those sizes appear: a refused buffer has to be able to be smaller.

The buffer is now made before the slot, by BufferCacheRuntime::TryCreateBuffer. SlotVector::insert is
noexcept in the source, so a try around it could not catch this on a desktop build, and the console's
copy throws (headless/slot_vector_recovery.cmake). Made first, neither matters. ResolveOverlaps has
picked the overlaps by then: they are unpicked, or the next lookups would skip them and register a
second buffer on their pages.

On a refusal, in order:

1. Without the stream leap: 8 MiB less for a buffer that keeps growing.
2. The range's first page is in a buffer: that buffer. It holds the start of the range.
3. Free pages from the start of the range up to the next buffer, as much as fits a hole
   (MemoryAllocator::CreateBufferInHoles, halving down to 64 KiB). No new block, no budget, no report,
   so a miss costs little. It is a normal cached buffer: a later request joins it.
4. The null buffer.

For 250 ms the allocator is not asked for the size refused or more, as in 0089: a refusal walks the
caches and writes to stderr, and the next draw is microseconds away. New buffers smaller than that
are still asked for.

CreateBuffer returns what is left, so every FindBuffer caller checks (buffer_cut). What that means:

- Uniform, storage, texture, vertex and transform feedback bindings: FindBinding shrinks the binding
  to what the buffer holds. The hardware bounds buffer descriptors, so reads past the end give zero
  and writes are dropped. With no buffer it becomes NULL_BINDING, the path of an unmapped address,
  which keeps size 0 off the null buffer (a range there is the VA 0 fault of the descriptor buffer
  fix). Storage sizes are often a guess (8 MiB for a native pointer), so the start is likely to be
  what the shader reads. Only the range kept is marked as GPU written.
- Index buffer, indirect arguments and count: the command processor reads those with no descriptor
  behind it, so all or nothing. No index buffer gives degenerate triangles, no arguments no draw and
  no dispatch.
- ObtainBuffer and ObtainCPUBuffer hand out the null buffer. SyncValues skips the copy: the CPU still
  has the final value (PopUnsyncedQueries), but a GPU reader sees the old one. Host conditional
  rendering ends and the draws go unconditionally, as on the drivers without it. A DMA image copy
  returns false, so it goes through guest memory.
- DMACopy and DMAClear return false and go through guest memory. Inline writes stay in guest memory
  and the range is marked CPU modified, so the next upload takes them.
- A multi range binding falls back to its one range. The inline index buffer goes to NULL_BINDING;
  erasing its slot as the code did would have erased the null buffer.

So the session goes on with missing geometry or effects where it used to end. A shader that gets
zeros may misdraw, and a guest waiting for a value a dropped write would have made could stall.

EDEN_NX2_BUFFER_CUT=0 ends the session as before. EDEN_BUFFER_REFUSED lines give the size refused and
the counts, one line per pause.

No lock is taken and nothing is submitted here, so this is safe with the cache mutexes held. The
scheduler is only recorded into, as for any new buffer.

Proven by reading the source and the generated copies: the slot and pick behaviour and every
FindBuffer caller. Proven with stubbed headers: the Vulkan and OpenGL template instantiations pass
clang -fsyntax-only with the same errors as before (only range_sets.inc, from the stubs), and
prepare-vulkan-port.py --report gives the same anchors. Not built, not run on a console. Likely: RADV
reads a vertex slot left empty as zero. A guess: how well a title looks in this state.

Sources: the refusal style of 0089, 0095 and 0096 and the EDEN_GRAPHICS_MEMORY reports; the null
descriptor fix in tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py; VMA_ALLOCATION_CREATE_NEVER_ALLOCATE_BIT in AMD's
Vulkan Memory Allocator; runs 51-53 of 2026-11-05.

